exercises.solution:
```python
def group_advantages(rewards):
    rewards = np.asarray(rewards, dtype=float)
    std = rewards.std()
    if std == 0:
        return np.zeros_like(rewards)
    return (rewards - rewards.mean()) / std
```

In a group of four with one success, the success gets advantage $+\sqrt{3} \approx 1.73$ and each failure $-1/\sqrt{3} \approx -0.58$: a rare success is strongly reinforced. When the whole group agrees, the prompt contributes nothing, which is why RLVR pipelines filter for prompts the model sometimes but not always solves. (Some later variants drop the division by the standard deviation, arguing it over-weights very easy and very hard prompts.)

