exercises.solution:
```python
def lora_forward(x, W0, A, B, alpha):
    r = A.shape[0]
    return x @ W0.T + (alpha / r) * ((x @ A.T) @ B.T)

def merge(W0, A, B, alpha):
    r = A.shape[0]
    return W0 + (alpha / r) * (B @ A)
```

The adapter path costs $O(n r (k + d))$ instead of $O(n k d)$, and only $A$ and $B$ need gradients. With $B = 0$ at initialisation the model is exactly the pretrained one, so fine-tuning starts from a known-good point. The $\alpha / r$ scaling keeps the update's size roughly stable when you change $r$, so you don't have to retune the learning rate each time.
